# Ujjwal Kumar

**PhD Candidate (3rd Year), Department of Mechanical Engineering, IIT Madras**

I am a PhD candidate in Mechanical Engineering at IIT Madras working in the field of cryogenic fluid dynamics, parametric sloshing, and multiphase flow instabilities. My research focuses on understanding fluid-interface dynamics in spacecraft propellant tanks through experimental investigations, high-speed diagnostics, and computational simulations.

My current doctoral research, conducted in collaboration with LPSC-ISRO, investigates sloshing-induced instabilities under high-frequency excitation relevant to space propulsion systems and cryogenic propellant management. The work includes experiments with liquid nitrogen, water, and synthetic fluids to study droplet ejection, gravity-capillary transitions, and nonlinear wave dynamics.

Previously, I completed an M.Tech in Cryogenic and Vacuum Technology from NIT Rourkela, where I worked on the design and performance analysis of high-speed helium expansion turbines during an internship at the Institute for Plasma Research (IPR), Gandhinagar.

